WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.087
Phone numbers are often stored with parentheses, spaces, periods, dashes, or other formatting.

00:00:06.087 --> 00:00:10.822
The first useful step is to separate normalization from validation.

00:00:10.822 --> 00:00:14.492
The query removes every character that is not a digit.

00:00:14.492 --> 00:00:18.002
That gives us a digits-only version of the phone number.

00:00:18.002 --> 00:00:21.976
Then we ask whether the result has exactly ten digits.

00:00:22.461 --> 00:00:30.833
For example, a phone written as three three six, with punctuation around the area code and between the number groups,

00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:36.583
can normalize to the same ten digits as a phone written with only digits.

00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:43.048
If the normalized value has ten digits, we keep it as a valid-shaped candidate.

00:00:43.048 --> 00:00:47.503
Otherwise, we return null for the validated field.

00:00:47.503 --> 00:00:52.843
Again, ten digits do not prove that the number belongs to the customer.

00:00:52.843 --> 00:00:56.034
They only satisfy the technical rule we selected.
